#: src/features/RolePlay/scenarios/talking-to-a-doctor.ts:16
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Talking to a doctor can be difficult, especially when you need to describe "
"symptoms in another language. You may need to explain what you feel, when "
"the problem started, how serious it is, and how it affects your daily life.\n"
"\n"
"You do not need advanced medical vocabulary. Clear descriptions, specific "
"examples, and honest answers are usually more useful than trying to guess "
"the name of a condition.\n"
"\n"
"This roleplay is for language practice only. It cannot diagnose a medical "
"condition, recommend treatment, or replace professional medical care. If "
"you believe you may be experiencing a medical emergency, contact your local "
"emergency service.\n"
"\n"
"## Start the Consultation\n"
"\n"
"A doctor may begin by asking:\n"
"\n"
"- “What brings you in today?”\n"
"- “How can I help you?”\n"
"- “What seems to be the problem?”\n"
"- “Could you describe your symptoms?”\n"
"- “When did this begin?”\n"
"- “Have you experienced this before?”\n"
"\n"
"You can begin with a short summary:\n"
"\n"
"- “I’ve had a persistent cough for about a week.”\n"
"- “I’ve been experiencing pain in my lower back.”\n"
"- “I’ve been feeling unusually tired recently.”\n"
"- “I have a rash on my arm that is not improving.”\n"
"- “I’ve been getting headaches more often than usual.”\n"
"- “I’m here to discuss the results of my recent tests.”\n"
"- “I’m following up about a problem we discussed previously.”\n"
"\n"
"A useful opening structure is:\n"
"\n"
"> “I’ve been experiencing [symptom] for [length of time], and it is "
"[improving, getting worse, or staying the same].”\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “I’ve been experiencing pain in my right shoulder for about ten days, and "
"it seems to be getting worse.”\n"
"\n"
"## Describe Where the Problem Is\n"
"\n"
"Be as specific as possible about the location of pain or discomfort.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “The pain is in my lower back.”\n"
"- “It hurts on the right side of my chest.”\n"
"- “I feel discomfort behind my left knee.”\n"
"- “The pain starts in my neck and moves into my shoulder.”\n"
"- “The rash is mainly on my arms.”\n"
"- “My whole body feels sore.”\n"
"- “It is difficult to identify the exact location.”\n"
"- “The pain seems to move.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Can you point to where it hurts?”\n"
"- “Is it on one side or both sides?”\n"
"- “Does the pain move anywhere else?”\n"
"- “Is the discomfort near the surface or deeper inside?”\n"
"- “Is the affected area sensitive to touch?”\n"
"\n"
"## Explain When It Started\n"
"\n"
"The doctor will often ask when the symptoms began and whether anything "
"happened beforehand.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “It started this morning.”\n"
"- “I first noticed it three days ago.”\n"
"- “The symptoms began gradually.”\n"
"- “It started suddenly while I was exercising.”\n"
"- “It began after I returned from a trip.”\n"
"- “I noticed it after starting a new medication.”\n"
"- “I woke up with the pain.”\n"
"- “I cannot remember exactly when it started.”\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions you may hear:\n"
"\n"
"- “Did it begin suddenly or gradually?”\n"
"- “What were you doing when it started?”\n"
"- “Did anything unusual happen beforehand?”\n"
"- “Have the symptoms changed since then?”\n"
"- “Was there an injury?”\n"
"\n"
"## Describe How It Feels\n"
"\n"
"Try to describe the sensation rather than only saying that something hurts.\n"
"\n"
"Pain or discomfort may feel:\n"
"\n"
"- Sharp\n"
"- Dull\n"
"- Burning\n"
"- Throbbing\n"
"- Stabbing\n"
"- Aching\n"
"- Cramping\n"
"- Tight\n"
"- Heavy\n"
"- Tingling\n"
"- Numb\n"
"- Itchy\n"
"- Sensitive\n"
"- Tender\n"
"\n"
"Examples:\n"
"\n"
"- “It feels like a dull ache.”\n"
"- “I sometimes feel a sharp pain.”\n"
"- “There is a burning sensation.”\n"
"- “My fingers feel numb.”\n"
"- “The area is itchy and sensitive.”\n"
"- “It feels like pressure rather than pain.”\n"
"- “My chest feels tight.”\n"
"- “I have a tingling sensation in my hand.”\n"
"\n"
"If you do not know the exact word, describe it in another way:\n"
"\n"
"> “It feels as if something is pressing on the area.”\n"
"\n"
"## Explain How Severe It Is\n"
"\n"
"A doctor may ask you to rate pain or discomfort on a scale from zero to "
"ten.\n"
"\n"
"- **Zero** means no pain.\n"
"- **Ten** means the worst pain you can imagine.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “The pain is about a four out of ten.”\n"
"- “It is mild most of the time.”\n"
"- “It becomes severe in the evening.”\n"
"- “It is uncomfortable, but I can still work.”\n"
"- “The pain is strong enough to wake me up.”\n"
"- “It is difficult to walk because of it.”\n"
"- “The symptoms interfere with my daily activities.”\n"
"\n"
"It is also helpful to explain what you can or cannot do:\n"
"\n"
"- “I can walk, but climbing stairs is painful.”\n"
"- “I cannot concentrate at work.”\n"
"- “I have difficulty sleeping.”\n"
"- “I can eat normally.”\n"
"- “I become tired after minor physical activity.”\n"
"- “I have had to stop exercising.”\n"
"\n"
"## Explain How Often It Happens\n"
"\n"
"Symptoms may be constant or may come and go.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “The pain is constant.”\n"
"- “It comes and goes.”\n"
"- “It happens several times a day.”\n"
"- “It usually lasts for about twenty minutes.”\n"
"- “The symptoms are worse in the morning.”\n"
"- “It happens mainly after eating.”\n"
"- “It only happens when I exercise.”\n"
"- “I feel better after resting.”\n"
"- “There does not seem to be a pattern.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “How long does each episode last?”\n"
"- “Does it happen every day?”\n"
"- “Is it worse at a particular time?”\n"
"- “Does anything trigger it?”\n"
"- “Does anything make it better?”\n"
"\n"
"## Describe What Makes It Better or Worse\n"
"\n"
"Explain whether movement, food, rest, medication, or another factor changes "
"your symptoms.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “It gets worse when I move.”\n"
"- “The pain increases when I take a deep breath.”\n"
"- “It feels better after I rest.”\n"
"- “Heat seems to help.”\n"
"- “The symptoms are worse after meals.”\n"
"- “It improves when I lie down.”\n"
"- “Pain relief helped temporarily.”\n"
"- “Nothing seems to make a difference.”\n"
"- “The problem becomes worse when I am stressed.”\n"
"\n"
"Useful vocabulary:\n"
"\n"
"- **Trigger** — something that causes or worsens a symptom\n"
"- **Relieve** — make a symptom less severe\n"
"- **Aggravate** — make a symptom worse\n"
"\n"
"## Mention Other Symptoms\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask whether you have noticed any other changes.\n"
"\n"
"Common questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do you have a fever?”\n"
"- “Have you felt sick or vomited?”\n"
"- “Have you noticed any swelling?”\n"
"- “Have you lost your appetite?”\n"
"- “Have you experienced dizziness?”\n"
"- “Are you short of breath?”\n"
"- “Have you had difficulty sleeping?”\n"
"- “Have you noticed any changes in your weight?”\n"
"- “Do you have any weakness or numbness?”\n"
"\n"
"You can respond:\n"
"\n"
"- “I also have a mild fever.”\n"
"- “I have felt dizzy a few times.”\n"
"- “There is some swelling around the area.”\n"
"- “I have not noticed any other symptoms.”\n"
"- “My appetite is normal.”\n"
"- “I have been sleeping poorly.”\n"
"- “I feel tired, but I do not have a fever.”\n"
"\n"
"Do not leave out a symptom because you think it is unrelated. The doctor "
"can decide whether it is relevant.\n"
"\n"
"## Explain What You Have Already Tried\n"
"\n"
"Tell the doctor whether you have taken medication, changed your routine, or "
"tried another solution.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I have been resting.”\n"
"- “I tried an over-the-counter pain reliever.”\n"
"- “I used a cold compress.”\n"
"- “I stopped exercising for several days.”\n"
"- “I changed my diet, but it did not help.”\n"
"- “The medication helped for a few hours.”\n"
"- “I have not tried anything yet.”\n"
"- “I spoke with a pharmacist before making this appointment.”\n"
"\n"
"Be ready to explain:\n"
"\n"
"- What you used\n"
"- How much you used\n"
"- How often you used it\n"
"- Whether it helped\n"
"- Whether you noticed side effects\n"
"\n"
"Do not pretend to remember details that you do not know. You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I don’t remember the exact name.”\n"
"- “I can show you the package.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure about the dosage.”\n"
"- “I took it according to the instructions on the label.”\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss Current Medication\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may need to know about all medication and supplements you take.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I take this medication every morning.”\n"
"- “I use an inhaler when necessary.”\n"
"- “I recently started taking a new prescription.”\n"
"- “I stopped taking it last week.”\n"
"- “I occasionally take an over-the-counter pain reliever.”\n"
"- “I’m not currently taking any medication.”\n"
"- “I have a list of my medications with me.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “What dose do you take?”\n"
"- “How long have you been taking it?”\n"
"- “Who prescribed it?”\n"
"- “Have you missed any doses?”\n"
"- “Have you noticed any side effects?”\n"
"\n"
"Do not stop prescribed medication based only on a language-learning "
"simulation. Discuss medication changes with a qualified healthcare "
"professional.\n"
"\n"
"## Mention Allergies\n"
"\n"
"Tell the doctor about medication, food, or other allergies.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “I’m allergic to penicillin.”\n"
"- “I have a severe peanut allergy.”\n"
"- “This medication caused a rash in the past.”\n"
"- “I do not have any known allergies.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure whether it was an allergic reaction.”\n"
"- “I experienced swelling after taking that medicine.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask what happened during the reaction and how serious it "
"was.\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss Your Medical History\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask about previous illnesses, injuries, operations, or "
"similar symptoms.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I had the same problem two years ago.”\n"
"- “I have a history of back problems.”\n"
"- “I had surgery five years ago.”\n"
"- “I was treated for this previously.”\n"
"- “I have not experienced this before.”\n"
"- “I have a long-term medical condition.”\n"
"- “I was recently in hospital.”\n"
"- “I have brought my previous test results.”\n"
"\n"
"You may also be asked about health conditions in your family:\n"
"\n"
"- “Does anyone in your family have a similar condition?”\n"
"- “Is there a family history of heart disease?”\n"
"- “Do any close relatives have diabetes?”\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “My father has a similar condition.”\n"
"- “There is no known family history.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure.”\n"
"- “I would need to check.”\n"
"\n"
"## Answer Lifestyle Questions\n"
"\n"
"A doctor may ask about sleep, exercise, diet, alcohol, smoking, work, or "
"stress because these factors can affect health.\n"
"\n"
"You may hear:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do you smoke?”\n"
"- “How often do you exercise?”\n"
"- “How much alcohol do you drink?”\n"
"- “How have you been sleeping?”\n"
"- “Has anything changed in your diet?”\n"
"- “Are you under unusual stress?”\n"
"- “What kind of work do you do?”\n"
"\n"
"Answer as honestly as possible. The purpose is to understand your health, "
"not to judge you.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “I do not smoke.”\n"
"- “I drink occasionally.”\n"
"- “I exercise two or three times a week.”\n"
"- “I sit at a desk for most of the day.”\n"
"- “I have been sleeping less than usual.”\n"
"- “My work has been particularly stressful recently.”\n"
"- “There have been no major lifestyle changes.”\n"
"\n"
"## Ask the Doctor to Clarify\n"
"\n"
"Medical explanations may contain unfamiliar words. It is always acceptable "
"to ask for simpler language.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “Could you explain that in simpler terms?”\n"
"- “What does that word mean?”\n"
"- “Could you repeat that more slowly?”\n"
"- “Could you write the name down?”\n"
"- “I’m not sure I understood.”\n"
"- “Does that mean the problem is serious?”\n"
"- “Could you show me where that is?”\n"
"- “Could you explain what you are checking for?”\n"
"- “Could you give me the instructions in writing?”\n"
"\n"
"To check your understanding, summarize what you heard:\n"
"\n"
"> “So you are saying that we need more information before we know what is "
"causing the problem. Is that correct?”\n"
"\n"
"## Understand Examinations and Tests\n"
"\n"
"During a consultation, a doctor may suggest an examination or test to "
"gather more information.\n"
"\n"
"Common examples include:\n"
"\n"
"- A physical examination\n"
"- A blood test\n"
"- A urine test\n"
"- A swab\n"
"- An X-ray\n"
"- An ultrasound\n"
"- Another type of scan\n"
"- A referral to a specialist\n"
"- Monitoring the symptoms for a period of time\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “What is this test for?”\n"
"- “How should I prepare?”\n"
"- “Will it be painful?”\n"
"- “Are there any risks?”\n"
"- “How long will it take?”\n"
"- “When will the results be available?”\n"
"- “How will I receive the results?”\n"
"- “Do I need another appointment?”\n"
"- “What happens if the result is abnormal?”\n"
"- “Is there an alternative?”\n"
"\n"
"The AI roleplay should only help you practise these conversations. It "
"should not claim that a particular test is medically necessary for your "
"real symptoms.\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss a Possible Plan\n"
"\n"
"A real doctor may explain possible next steps, but you should ask questions "
"before agreeing to anything you do not understand.\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “What are the next steps?”\n"
"- “What are the available options?”\n"
"- “What is the purpose of this medication?”\n"
"- “How should it be taken?”\n"
"- “Are there common side effects?”\n"
"- “Could it interact with my other medication?”\n"
"- “How soon should I expect an improvement?”\n"
"- “What should I do if it does not help?”\n"
"- “Is there anything I should avoid?”\n"
"- “Do I need to change my normal activities?”\n"
"\n"
"You can also ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Are there any alternatives?”\n"
"- “What would happen if we waited?”\n"
"- “Do I need to decide today?”\n"
"- “Could I have some written information?”\n"
"- “Should I speak with a specialist?”\n"
"\n"
"The roleplay may practise asking these questions, but it must not prescribe "
"medication or create a real treatment plan.\n"
"\n"
"## Confirm Medication Instructions\n"
"\n"
"If a real clinician prescribes medication, confirm exactly how it should be "
"used.\n"
"\n"
"You may need to understand:\n"
"\n"
"- The medication name\n"
"- The dose\n"
"- How often to take it\n"
"- When to take it\n"
"- How long to take it\n"
"- Whether to take it with food\n"
"- Possible side effects\n"
"- What to do if you miss a dose\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “How often should I take it?”\n"
"- “Should I take it before or after food?”\n"
"- “How many days should I use it?”\n"
"- “What should I do if I miss a dose?”\n"
"- “Are there any side effects I should watch for?”\n"
"- “Can I take it with my other medication?”\n"
"- “Could you write the instructions down?”\n"
"\n"
"Do not rely on the roleplay for real dosing instructions. Follow the "
"directions given by your doctor or pharmacist.\n"
"\n"
"## Ask When to Seek More Help\n"
"\n"
"Before leaving, ask what to do if the symptoms continue or become worse.\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “When should I contact the clinic again?”\n"
"- “What should I do if the symptoms get worse?”\n"
"- “Which changes should I watch for?”\n"
"- “When should I arrange a follow-up?”\n"
"- “Who should I contact outside normal hours?”\n"
"- “Should I go to urgent care if this happens again?”\n"
"- “How long should I wait before asking for another appointment?”\n"
"\n"
"A real healthcare professional can give safety instructions based on your "
"specific situation. A simulation cannot assess your personal medical risk.\n"
"\n"
"## Request a Referral or Follow-Up\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may recommend another appointment or referral.\n"
"\n"
"You can ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do I need to see a specialist?”\n"
"- “Will you send the referral?”\n"
"- “Do I need to contact the specialist myself?”\n"
"- “How long does the referral usually take?”\n"
"- “Should I book a follow-up appointment?”\n"
"- “When should I come back?”\n"
"- “Will we discuss the test results at the next visit?”\n"
"- “Can the follow-up be done by phone?”\n"
"\n"
"Before leaving, confirm who is responsible for the next action.\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss Privacy or Sensitive Topics\n"
"\n"
"Some health concerns may be difficult to discuss.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “This is difficult for me to talk about.”\n"
"- “I would prefer to discuss this privately.”\n"
"- “Could the other person leave the room?”\n"
"- “Could I speak with a doctor of a particular gender?”\n"
"- “I need a moment before I continue.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure how to describe the problem.”\n"
"- “Could I write it down instead?”\n"
"\n"
"You can also ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Will this information remain confidential?”\n"
"- “Who will have access to my medical information?”\n"
"- “Can I bring someone I trust into the consultation?”\n"
"\n"
"## Take Notes and Check Your Understanding\n"
"\n"
"It can be difficult to remember everything discussed during an appointment.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do you mind if I take notes?”\n"
"- “Could you write down the important instructions?”\n"
"- “Could you repeat the name of the test?”\n"
"- “Can I receive a summary after the appointment?”\n"
"- “Let me make sure I understood correctly.”\n"
"\n"
"A useful method is to repeat the plan in your own words:\n"
"\n"
"> “So I need to complete the test, continue tracking the symptoms, and "
"arrange another appointment after the results are available. Is that "
"correct?”\n"
"\n"
"This technique helps identify misunderstandings before you leave.\n"
"\n"
"## A Simple Doctor Conversation\n"
"\n"
"A typical consultation may look like this:\n"
"\n"
"> **Doctor:** What brings you in today?  \n"
"> **Patient:** I’ve had pain in my lower back for about a week, and it is "
"not improving.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** Did it begin suddenly or gradually?  \n"
"> **Patient:** Gradually. I first noticed it after working at my desk for "
"several hours.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** How would you describe the pain?  \n"
"> **Patient:** It is usually a dull ache, but I sometimes feel a sharp pain "
"when I stand up.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** How severe is it?  \n"
"> **Patient:** About five out of ten. It is worse in the evening.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** Have you tried anything for it?  \n"
"> **Patient:** I have been resting, but that has not made much difference.  "
"\n"
"> **Doctor:** Do you have any other symptoms?  \n"
"> **Patient:** No, I have not noticed anything else.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** Thank you. I would now ask some additional questions and "
"explain what a clinician might examine.  \n"
"> **Patient:** Before we continue, could you explain what you are checking "
"for?\n"
"\n"
"## A Simple Symptom Formula\n"
"\n"
"When describing a health concern, include:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Main symptom:** What is bothering you?\n"
"2. **Location:** Where do you feel it?\n"
"3. **Onset:** When and how did it begin?\n"
"4. **Description:** What does it feel like?\n"
"5. **Severity:** How strong or disruptive is it?\n"
"6. **Pattern:** Is it constant, occasional, improving, or worsening?\n"
"7. **Triggers:** What makes it better or worse?\n"
"8. **Other symptoms:** Have you noticed anything else?\n"
"9. **Previous attempts:** What have you already tried?\n"
"10. **Questions:** What do you want the doctor to explain?\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “I’ve had a sharp pain in my right shoulder for ten days. It is about six "
"out of ten and becomes worse when I lift my arm. Rest helps a little, but "
"the problem is not improving.”\n"
"\n"
"## Practice Scenario\n"
"\n"
"In this roleplay, you are attending an appointment with Dr. Ash. The AI "
"doctor will help you practise describing a health concern, answering common "
"questions, and understanding the structure of a medical consultation.\n"
"\n"
"During the conversation, try to:\n"
"\n"
"- Give a short summary of your main concern.\n"
"- Explain when the symptoms started.\n"
"- Describe their location, severity, and frequency.\n"
"- Say what makes them better or worse.\n"
"- Mention any related symptoms.\n"
"- Explain what you have already tried.\n"
"- Answer general questions about medication, allergies, and medical "
"history.\n"
"- Ask for clarification when you do not understand something.\n"
"- Ask what a real patient might need to confirm about tests or next steps.\n"
"- Summarize the conversation in your own words before it ends.\n"
"\n"
"Focus on describing your experience clearly. Do not use this simulation to "
"obtain a diagnosis, medication recommendation, or treatment plan."
msgstr ""
"Hablar con un médico puede ser difícil, especialmente cuando necesitas "
"describir síntomas en otro idioma. Puede que necesites explicar lo que "
"sientes, cuándo comenzó el problema, qué tan grave es y cómo afecta tu vida "
"diaria.\n"
"\n"
"No necesitas tener un vocabulario médico avanzado. Descripciones claras, "
"ejemplos específicos y respuestas honestas son usualmente más útiles que "
"intentar adivinar el nombre de una condición.\n"
"\n"
"Este juego de roles es solo para practicar el idioma. No puede diagnosticar "
"una condición médica, recomendar un tratamiento ni reemplazar la atención "
"médica profesional. Si crees que puedes estar experimentando una emergencia "
"médica, contacta a tu servicio de emergencia local.\n"
"\n"
"## Iniciar la Consulta\n"
"\n"
"Un médico puede comenzar preguntando:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Qué te trae por aquí hoy?”\n"
"- “¿Cómo puedo ayudarte?”\n"
"- “¿Cuál parece ser el problema?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías describir tus síntomas?”\n"
"- “¿Cuándo comenzó esto?”\n"
"- “¿Has experimentado esto antes?”\n"
"\n"
"Puedes comenzar con un resumen corto:\n"
"\n"
"- “He tenido una tos persistente durante aproximadamente una semana.”\n"
"- “He estado sintiendo dolor en la parte baja de mi espalda.”\n"
"- “Me he sentido inusualmente cansado recientemente.”\n"
"- “Tengo una erupción en mi brazo que no está mejorando.”\n"
"- “He estado teniendo dolores de cabeza más frecuentemente de lo habitual.”\n"
"- “Estoy aquí para discutir los resultados de mis pruebas recientes.”\n"
"- “Estoy haciendo seguimiento sobre un problema que discutimos "
"anteriormente.”\n"
"\n"
"Una estructura de apertura útil es:\n"
"\n"
"> “He estado experimentando [síntoma] durante [tiempo], y está [mejorando, "
"empeorando o permaneciendo igual].”\n"
"\n"
"Por ejemplo:\n"
"\n"
"> “He estado sintiendo dolor en mi hombro derecho durante aproximadamente "
"diez días, y parece estar empeorando.”\n"
"\n"
"## Describir Dónde Está el Problema\n"
"\n"
"Sé lo más específico posible sobre la ubicación del dolor o la incomodidad.\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “El dolor está en mi parte baja de la espalda.”\n"
"- “Duele en el lado derecho de mi pecho.”\n"
"- “Siento malestar detrás de mi rodilla izquierda.”\n"
"- “El dolor comienza en mi cuello y se mueve hacia mi hombro.”\n"
"- “La erupción está principalmente en mis brazos.”\n"
"- “Todo mi cuerpo se siente adolorido.”\n"
"- “Es difícil identificar la ubicación exacta.”\n"
"- “El dolor parece moverse.”\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Puedes señalar dónde duele?”\n"
"- “¿Está en un lado o en ambos lados?”\n"
"- “¿Se mueve el dolor a alguna otra parte?”\n"
"- “¿Está la incomodidad cerca de la superficie o más adentro?”\n"
"- “¿Está la zona afectada sensible al tacto?”\n"
"\n"
"## Explicar Cuándo Comenzó\n"
"\n"
"El médico a menudo preguntará cuándo comenzaron los síntomas y si sucedió "
"algo antes.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “Comenzó esta mañana.”\n"
"- “Lo noté por primera vez hace tres días.”\n"
"- “Los síntomas comenzaron gradualmente.”\n"
"- “Comenzó repentinamente mientras hacía ejercicio.”\n"
"- “Comenzó después de regresar de un viaje.”\n"
"- “Lo noté después de comenzar un nuevo medicamento.”\n"
"- “Me desperté con el dolor.”\n"
"- “No recuerdo exactamente cuándo comenzó.”\n"
"\n"
"Preguntas útiles que puedes escuchar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Comenzó repentinamente o gradualmente?”\n"
"- “¿Qué estabas haciendo cuando comenzó?”\n"
"- “¿Sucedió algo inusual antes?”\n"
"- “¿Han cambiado los síntomas desde entonces?”\n"
"- “¿Hubo alguna lesión?”\n"
"\n"
"## Describir Cómo Se Siente\n"
"\n"
"Trata de describir la sensación en lugar de solo decir que algo duele.\n"
"\n"
"El dolor o la incomodidad pueden sentirse:\n"
"\n"
"- Agudo\n"
"- Sordo\n"
"- Quemante\n"
"- Pulsante\n"
"- Punza\n"
"- Dolor profundo\n"
"- Calambres\n"
"- Apretado\n"
"- Pesado\n"
"- Hormigueo\n"
"- Entumecido\n"
"- Pica\n"
"- Sensible\n"
"- Tierno\n"
"\n"
"Ejemplos:\n"
"\n"
"- “Se siente como un dolor sordo.”\n"
"- “A veces siento un dolor agudo.”\n"
"- “Hay una sensación de ardor.”\n"
"- “Mis dedos se sienten entumecidos.”\n"
"- “El área está sensible y pica.”\n"
"- “Se siente como presión en lugar de dolor.”\n"
"- “Mi pecho se siente apretado.”\n"
"- “Tengo una sensación de hormigueo en mi mano.”\n"
"\n"
"Si no conoces la palabra exacta, descríbelo de otra manera:\n"
"\n"
"> “Se siente como si algo estuviera presionando en el área.”\n"
"\n"
"## Explicar Qué Tan Grave Es\n"
"\n"
"Un médico puede pedirte que califiques el dolor o la incomodidad en una "
"escala del cero al diez.\n"
"\n"
"- **Cero** significa sin dolor.\n"
"- **Diez** significa el peor dolor que puedas imaginar.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “El dolor es de alrededor de cuatro de diez.”\n"
"- “Es leve la mayor parte del tiempo.”\n"
"- “Se vuelve severo por la noche.”\n"
"- “Es incómodo, pero aún puedo trabajar.”\n"
"- “El dolor es lo suficientemente fuerte como para despertarme.”\n"
"- “Es difícil caminar por esto.”\n"
"- “Los síntomas interfieren con mis actividades diarias.”\n"
"\n"
"También es útil explicar lo que puedes o no puedes hacer:\n"
"\n"
"- “Puedo caminar, pero subir escaleras es doloroso.”\n"
"- “No puedo concentrarme en el trabajo.”\n"
"- “Tengo dificultades para dormir.”\n"
"- “Puedo comer normalmente.”\n"
"- “Me canso después de actividades físicas menores.”\n"
"- “He tenido que dejar de hacer ejercicio.”\n"
"\n"
"## Explicar Con Qué Frecuencia Sucede\n"
"\n"
"Los síntomas pueden ser constantes o pueden ir y venir.\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “El dolor es constante.”\n"
"- “Va y viene.”\n"
"- “Sucede varias veces al día.”\n"
"- “Usualmente dura unos veinte minutos.”\n"
"- “Los síntomas son peores en la mañana.”\n"
"- “Sucede principalmente después de comer.”\n"
"- “Solo sucede cuando hago ejercicio.”\n"
"- “Me siento mejor después de descansar.”\n"
"- “No parece haber un patrón.”\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Cuánto dura cada episodio?”\n"
"- “¿Sucede todos los días?”\n"
"- “¿Es peor a una hora en particular?”\n"
"- “¿Algo lo desencadena?”\n"
"- “¿Algo lo mejora?”\n"
"\n"
"## Describir Qué Lo Mejora o Empeora\n"
"\n"
"Explica si el movimiento, la comida, el descanso, el medicamento u otro "
"factor cambian tus síntomas.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “Empeora cuando me muevo.”\n"
"- “El dolor aumenta cuando respiro profundamente.”\n"
"- “Se siente mejor después de descansar.”\n"
"- “El calor parece ayudar.”\n"
"- “Los síntomas son peores después de las comidas.”\n"
"- “Mejora cuando me acuesto.”\n"
"- “El alivio del dolor ayudó temporalmente.”\n"
"- “Nada parece hacer la diferencia.”\n"
"- “El problema empeora cuando estoy estresado.”\n"
"\n"
"Vocabulaboio útil:\n"
"\n"
"- **Desencadenante** — algo que causa o empeora un síntoma\n"
"- **Aliviar** — hacer que un síntoma sea menos severo\n"
"- **Agravar** — empeorar un síntoma\n"
"\n"
"## Mencionar Otros Síntomas\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede preguntar si has notado algún otro cambio.\n"
"\n"
"Las preguntas comunes incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Tienes fiebre?”\n"
"- “¿Te has sentido enfermo o has vomitado?”\n"
"- “¿Has notado alguna hinchazón?”\n"
"- “¿Has perdido el apetito?”\n"
"- “¿Has experimentado mareos?”\n"
"- “¿Tienes dificultad para respirar?”\n"
"- “¿Has tenido dificultad para dormir?”\n"
"- “¿Has notado algún cambio en tu peso?”\n"
"- “¿Tienes debilidad o adormecimiento?”\n"
"\n"
"Puedes responder:\n"
"\n"
"- “También tengo una fiebre leve.”\n"
"- “Me he sentido mareado algunas veces.”\n"
"- “Hay algo de hinchazón alrededor del área.”\n"
"- “No he notado otros síntomas.”\n"
"- “Mi apetito es normal.”\n"
"- “He estado durmiendo mal.”\n"
"- “Me siento cansado, pero no tengo fiebre.”\n"
"\n"
"No omitas un síntoma porque creas que no está relacionado. El médico puede "
"decidir si es relevante.\n"
"\n"
"## Explicar Qué Ya Has Intentado\n"
"\n"
"Dile al médico si has tomado medicación, cambiado tu rutina o intentado "
"otra solución.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “He estado descansando.”\n"
"- “Intenté un analgésico de venta libre.”\n"
"- “Usé una compresa fría.”\n"
"- “Dejé de hacer ejercicio por varios días.”\n"
"- “Cambié mi dieta, pero no ayudó.”\n"
"- “La medicación ayudó por unas horas.”\n"
"- “No he intentado nada aún.”\n"
"- “Hablé con un farmacéutico antes de hacer esta cita.”\n"
"\n"
"Prepárate para explicar:\n"
"\n"
"- Qué usaste\n"
"- Cuánto usaste\n"
"- Con qué frecuencia lo usaste\n"
"- Si ayudó\n"
"- Si notaste efectos secundarios\n"
"\n"
"No pretendas recordar detalles que no conoces. Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “No recuerdo el nombre exacto.”\n"
"- “Puedo mostrarte el paquete.”\n"
"- “No estoy seguro de la dosis.”\n"
"- “Lo tomé según las instrucciones en la etiqueta.”\n"
"\n"
"## Discutir Medicación Actual\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede necesitar saber sobre todos los medicamentos y suplementos "
"que tomas.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “Tomo este medicamento cada mañana.”\n"
"- “Uso un inhalador cuando es necesario.”\n"
"- “Recientemente comencé a tomar una nueva prescrición.”\n"
"- “Dejé de tomarlo la semana pasada.”\n"
"- “Ocasionalmente tomo un analgésico de venta libre.”\n"
"- “Actualmente no estoy tomando ninguna medicación.”\n"
"- “Tengo una lista de mis medicamentos conmigo.”\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Qué dosis tomas?”\n"
"- “¿Cuánto tiempo llevas tomándolo?”\n"
"- “¿Quién lo recetó?”\n"
"- “¿Te has saltado alguna dosis?”\n"
"- “¿Has notado algún efecto secundario?”\n"
"\n"
"No dejes de tomar la medicación prescrita solo por una simulación de "
"aprendizaje de idiomas. Habla sobre cambios en la medicación con un "
"profesional de salud calificado.\n"
"\n"
"## Mencionar Alergias\n"
"\n"
"Dile al médico sobre alergias a medicamentos, alimentos u otras cosas.\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “Soy alérgico a la penicilina.”\n"
"- “Tengo una alergia severa a los cacahuetes.”\n"
"- “Este medicamento causó una erupción en el pasado.”\n"
"- “No tengo alergias conocidas.”\n"
"- “No estoy seguro si fue una reacción alérgica.”\n"
"- “Experimenté hinchazón después de tomar ese medicamento.”\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede preguntar qué sucedió durante la reacción y qué tan grave "
"fue.\n"
"\n"
"## Discutir Tu Historial Médico\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede preguntar sobre enfermedades previas, lesiones, operaciones "
"o síntomas similares.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “Tuve el mismo problema hace dos años.”\n"
"- “Tengo historial de problemas de espalda.”\n"
"- “Tuve una cirugía hace cinco años.”\n"
"- “Fui tratado por esto anteriormente.”\n"
"- “No he experimentado esto antes.”\n"
"- “Tengo una condición médica a largo plazo.”\n"
"- “Recientemente estuve en el hospital.”\n"
"- “He traído mis resultados de pruebas anteriores.”\n"
"\n"
"También puedes ser preguntado sobre condiciones de salud en tu familia:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Alguien en tu familia tiene una condición similar?”\n"
"- “¿Hay historial familiar de enfermedades del corazón?”\n"
"- “¿Algún pariente cercano tiene diabetes?”\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “Mi padre tiene una condición similar.”\n"
"- “No hay historial familiar conocido.”\n"
"- “No estoy seguro.”\n"
"- “Tendría que verificar.”\n"
"\n"
"## Responder Preguntas sobre el Estilo de Vida\n"
"\n"
"Un médico puede preguntar sobre el sueño, ejercicio, dieta, alcohol, fumar, "
"trabajo o estrés porque estos factores pueden afectar la salud.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes escuchar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Fumas?”\n"
"- “¿Con qué frecuencia haces ejercicio?”\n"
"- “¿Cuánto alcohol bebes?”\n"
"- “¿Cómo has estado durmiendo?”\n"
"- “¿Ha cambiado algo en tu dieta?”\n"
"- “¿Estás bajo estrés inusual?”\n"
"- “¿Qué tipo de trabajo haces?”\n"
"\n"
"Responde lo más honestamente posible. El propósito es entender tu salud, no "
"juzgarte.\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “No fumo.”\n"
"- “Bebo ocasionalmente.”\n"
"- “Hago ejercicio dos o tres veces por semana.”\n"
"- “Me siento en un escritorio la mayor parte del día.”\n"
"- “He estado durmiendo menos de lo habitual.”\n"
"- “Mi trabajo ha sido particularmente estresante recientemente.”\n"
"- “No ha habido cambios importantes en mi estilo de vida.”\n"
"\n"
"## Pedir al Médico que Aclare\n"
"\n"
"Las explicaciones médicas pueden contener palabras desconocidas. Siempre es "
"aceptable pedir un lenguaje más simple.\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Podrías explicar eso en términos más simples?”\n"
"- “¿Qué significa esa palabra?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías repetir eso más despacio?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías escribir el nombre?”\n"
"- “No estoy seguro de haber entendido.”\n"
"- “¿Eso significa que el problema es serio?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías mostrarme dónde está eso?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías explicar qué estás verificando?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías darme las instrucciones por escrito?”\n"
"\n"
"Para verificar tu comprensión, resume lo que escuchaste:\n"
"\n"
"> “Entonces, estás diciendo que necesitamos más información antes de saber "
"qué está causando el problema. ¿Es correcto?”\n"
"\n"
"## Entender los Exámenes y Pruebas\n"
"\n"
"Durante una consulta, un médico puede sugerir un examen o prueba para "
"reunir más información.\n"
"\n"
"Ejemplos comunes incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- Un examen físico\n"
"- Una prueba de sangre\n"
"- Una prueba de orina\n"
"- Un hisopado\n"
"- Una radiografía\n"
"- Un ultrasonido\n"
"- Otro tipo de escaneo\n"
"- Una referencia a un especialista\n"
"- Monitoreo de los síntomas por un periodo de tiempo\n"
"\n"
"Preguntas útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Para qué es esta prueba?”\n"
"- “¿Cómo debería prepararme?”\n"
"- “¿Será doloroso?”\n"
"- “¿Hay riesgos?”\n"
"- “¿Cuánto tiempo llevará?”\n"
"- “¿Cuándo estarán disponibles los resultados?”\n"
"- “¿Cómo recibiré los resultados?”\n"
"- “¿Necesito otra cita?”\n"
"- “¿Qué sucede si el resultado es anormal?”\n"
"- “¿Hay una alternativa?”\n"
"\n"
"El juego de roles con la IA solo debe ayudarte a practicar estas "
"conversaciones. No debe afirmar que una prueba en particular es médicamente "
"necesaria para tus síntomas reales.\n"
"\n"
"## Discutir un Posible Plan\n"
"\n"
"Un médico real puede explicar los posibles siguientes pasos, pero debes "
"hacer preguntas antes de aceptar algo que no entiendas.\n"
"\n"
"Preguntas útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Cuáles son los próximos pasos?”\n"
"- “¿Cuáles son las opciones disponibles?”\n"
"- “¿Cuál es el propósito de este medicamento?”\n"
"- “¿Cómo debería tomarse?”\n"
"- “¿Hay efectos secundarios comunes?”\n"
"- “¿Podría interactuar con mi otra medicación?”\n"
"- “¿Qué tan pronto debería esperar una mejora?”\n"
"- “¿Qué debo hacer si no ayuda?”\n"
"- “¿Hay algo que deba evitar?”\n"
"- “¿Necesito cambiar mis actividades normales?”\n"
"\n"
"También puedes preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Hay alternativas?”\n"
"- “¿Qué pasaría si esperamos?”\n"
"- “¿Necesito decidir hoy?”\n"
"- “¿Podría tener información escrita?”\n"
"- “¿Debería hablar con un especialista?”\n"
"\n"
"El juego de roles puede practicar hacer estas preguntas, pero no debe "
"prescribir medicación ni crear un plan de tratamiento real.\n"
"\n"
"## Confirmar Instrucciones de Medicación\n"
"\n"
"Si un médico real prescribe medicación, confirma exactamente cómo debe "
"usarse.\n"
"\n"
"Es posible que necesites entender:\n"
"\n"
"- El nombre del medicamento\n"
"- La dosis\n"
"- Con qué frecuencia tomarlo\n"
"- Cuándo tomarlo\n"
"- Cuánto tiempo tomarlo\n"
"- Si debe tomarse con comida\n"
"- Posibles efectos secundarios\n"
"- Qué hacer si omites una dosis\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Con qué frecuencia debería tomarlo?”\n"
"- “¿Debo tomarlo antes o después de la comida?”\n"
"- “¿Cuántos días debería usarlo?”\n"
"- “¿Qué debo hacer si omito una dosis?”\n"
"- “¿Hay algún efecto secundario que deba vigilar?”\n"
"- “¿Puedo tomarlo con mi otra medicación?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías escribir las instrucciones?”\n"
"\n"
"No dependas del juego de roles para instrucciones de dosificación reales. "
"Sigue las instrucciones dadas por tu médico o farmacéutico.\n"
"\n"
"## Preguntar Cuándo Buscar Más Ayuda\n"
"\n"
"Antes de irte, pregunta qué hacer si los síntomas continúan o empeoran.\n"
"\n"
"Preguntas útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Cuándo debería contactar a la clínica de nuevo?”\n"
"- “¿Qué debo hacer si los síntomas empeoran?”\n"
"- “¿Qué cambios debo vigilar?”\n"
"- “¿Cuándo debo programar un seguimiento?”\n"
"- “¿Con quién debo contactar fuera del horario normal?”\n"
"- “¿Debería ir a urgencias si esto sucede de nuevo?”\n"
"- “¿Cuánto tiempo debería esperar antes de pedir otra cita?”\n"
"\n"
"Un profesional médico real puede dar instrucciones de seguridad basadas en "
"tu situación específica. Una simulación no puede evaluar tu riesgo médico "
"personal.\n"
"\n"
"## Solicitar una Referencia o Seguimiento\n"
"\n"
"El médico puede recomendar otra cita o referencia.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Necesito ver a un especialista?”\n"
"- “¿Enviarás tú la referencia?”\n"
"- “¿Necesito contactar al especialista yo mismo?”\n"
"- “¿Cuánto tiempo suele tardar la referencia?”\n"
"- “¿Debería programar una cita de seguimiento?”\n"
"- “¿Cuándo debería regresar?”\n"
"- “¿Discutiremos los resultados de las pruebas en la próxima visita?”\n"
"- “¿El seguimiento puede hacerse por teléfono?”\n"
"\n"
"Antes de irte, confirma quién es responsable de la próxima acción.\n"
"\n"
"## Discutir la Privacidad o Temas Sensibles\n"
"\n"
"Algunas preocupaciones de salud pueden ser difíciles de discutir.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “Esto es difícil para mí de hablar.”\n"
"- “Preferiría discutir esto en privado.”\n"
"- “¿Podría la otra persona salir de la habitación?”\n"
"- “¿Podría hablar con un médico de un género en particular?”\n"
"- “Necesito un momento antes de continuar.”\n"
"- “No estoy seguro de cómo describir el problema.”\n"
"- “¿Podría escribirlo en vez?”\n"
"\n"
"También puedes preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Esta información permanecerá confidencial?”\n"
"- “¿Quién tendrá acceso a mi información médica?”\n"
"- “¿Puede entrar alguien de confianza a la consulta?”\n"
"\n"
"## Tomar Notas y Verificar Tu Comprensión\n"
"\n"
"Puede ser difícil recordar todo lo discutido durante una cita.\n"
"\n"
"Puedes decir:\n"
"\n"
"- “¿Te importa si tomo notas?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías escribir las instrucciones importantes?”\n"
"- “¿Podrías repetir el nombre de la prueba?”\n"
"- “¿Puedo recibir un resumen después de la cita?”\n"
"- “Déjame asegurarme de entender correctamente.”\n"
"\n"
"Un método útil es repetir el plan con tus propias palabras:\n"
"\n"
"> “Entonces necesito completar la prueba, seguir rastreando los síntomas, y "
"programar otra cita después de que los resultados estén disponibles. ¿Es "
"correcto?”\n"
"\n"
"Esta técnica ayuda a identificar malentendidos antes de irte.\n"
"\n"
"## Una Conversación Simple con el Médico\n"
"\n"
"Una consulta típica puede verse así:\n"
"\n"
"> **Doctor:** ¿Qué te trae por aquí hoy?  \n"
"> **Paciente:** He tenido dolor en mi parte baja de la espalda durante "
"aproximadamente una semana, y no está mejorando.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** ¿Comenzó repentinamente o gradualmente?  \n"
"> **Paciente:** Gradualmente. Lo noté por primera vez después de trabajar "
"en mi escritorio por varias horas.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** ¿Cómo describirías el dolor?  \n"
"> **Paciente:** Usualmente es un dolor sordo, pero a veces siento un dolor "
"agudo cuando me levanto.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** ¿Qué tan severo es?  \n"
"> **Paciente:** Alrededor de cinco de diez. Es peor por la noche.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** ¿Has intentado algo para esto?  \n"
"> **Paciente:** He estado descansando, pero eso no ha hecho mucha "
"diferencia.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** ¿Tienes otros síntomas?  \n"
"> **Paciente:** No, no he notado nada más.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** Gracias. Ahora haré algunas preguntas adicionales y explicaré "
"lo que un clínico podría examinar.  \n"
"> **Paciente:** Antes de continuar, ¿podrías explicar qué estás "
"verificando?\n"
"\n"
"## Una Fórmula Simple para Describir Síntomas\n"
"\n"
"Cuando describes una preocupación de salud, incluye:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Síntoma principal:** ¿Qué te molesta?\n"
"2. **Ubicación:** ¿Dónde lo sientes?\n"
"3. **Comienzo:** ¿Cuándo y cómo comenzó?\n"
"4. **Descripción:** ¿Cómo se siente?\n"
"5. **Severidad:** ¿Qué tan fuerte o disruptivo es?\n"
"6. **Patrón:** ¿Es constante, ocasional, mejora o empeora?\n"
"7. **Desencadenantes:** ¿Qué lo mejora o empeora?\n"
"8. **Otros síntomas:** ¿Has notado algo más?\n"
"9. **Intentos previos:** ¿Qué ya has intentado?\n"
"10. **Preguntas:** ¿Qué quieres que el médico explique?\n"
"\n"
"Por ejemplo:\n"
"\n"
"> “He tenido un dolor agudo en mi hombro derecho durante diez días. Es de "
"alrededor de seis de diez y empeora cuando levanto mi brazo. Descansar "
"ayuda un poco, pero el problema no está mejorando.”\n"
"\n"
"## Escenario de Práctica\n"
"\n"
"En este juego de roles, asistes a una cita con el Dr. Ash. El médico de IA "
"te ayudará a practicar describiendo una preocupación de salud, respondiendo "
"preguntas comunes y entendiendo la estructura de una consulta médica.\n"
"\n"
"Durante la conversación, intenta:\n"
"\n"
"- Dar un breve resumen de tu preocupación principal.\n"
"- Explicar cuándo comenzaron los síntomas.\n"
"- Describir su ubicación, severidad y frecuencia.\n"
"- Decir qué los mejora o empeora.\n"
"- Mencionar cualquier síntoma relacionado.\n"
"- Explicar lo que ya has intentado.\n"
"- Responder preguntas generales sobre medicación, alergias e historial "
"médico.\n"
"- Pedir aclaraciones cuando no entiendas algo.\n"
"- Preguntar qué podría necesitar confirmar un paciente real sobre exámenes "
"o próximos pasos.\n"
"- Resumir la conversación en tus propias palabras antes de que termine.\n"
"\n"
"Enfócate en describir tu experiencia claramente. No uses esta simulación "
"para obtener un diagnóstico, recomendación de medicación o plan de "
"tratamiento."

#: src/features/RolePlay/scenarios/small-talk-with-a-stranger.ts:13
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Talking to a stranger can feel uncomfortable because neither person knows "
"what to say yet. The goal is not to sound especially clever or interesting. "
"Your first goal is simply to make the other person feel comfortable and "
"discover something you can talk about together.\n"
"\n"
"The advice below is inspired by ideas from Charles Duhigg’s book "
"*Supercommunicators*.\n"
"\n"
"## How to Start a Conversation\n"
"\n"
"Use the situation you already share. You do not need an original or "
"impressive opening line.\n"
"\n"
"Try making a simple observation and adding an easy question:\n"
"\n"
"- “This place is busier than I expected. Have you been here before?”\n"
"- “How do you know the host?”\n"
"- “What brought you to this event?”\n"
"- “That looks interesting. What are you drinking?”\n"
"- “Is this your first time at an event like this?”\n"
"\n"
"Questions about the immediate situation feel natural because they give the "
"other person an obvious way to respond.\n"
"\n"
"## Ask Questions That Reveal More Than Facts\n"
"\n"
"Basic questions are useful for starting a conversation:\n"
"\n"
"- “Where are you from?”\n"
"- “What do you do?”\n"
"- “Do you live nearby?”\n"
"\n"
"However, a conversation becomes more interesting when you gently ask about "
"the person’s experiences, feelings, or preferences.\n"
"\n"
"Instead of only asking:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you do for work?”\n"
"\n"
"You could continue with:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you enjoy most about it?”\n"
"\n"
"Instead of:\n"
"\n"
"> “How long have you lived here?”\n"
"\n"
"Try:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you like most about living here?”\n"
"\n"
"Other useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “How did you become interested in that?”\n"
"- “What has been the best part of your week?”\n"
"- “What do you usually enjoy doing on weekends?”\n"
"- “What is something you are looking forward to?”\n"
"- “What kind of places do you like visiting?”\n"
"\n"
"These questions are personal enough to create a connection, but still safe "
"and appropriate for someone you have just met.\n"
"\n"
"## Listen for the Kind of Conversation They Want\n"
"\n"
"People do not always want the same thing from a conversation. They may want "
"to:\n"
"\n"
"1. Exchange information or make a decision.\n"
"2. Share an emotion or experience.\n"
"3. Talk about their interests, identity, or relationships.\n"
"\n"
"Try to respond to the kind of conversation the other person has started.\n"
"\n"
"If someone says:\n"
"\n"
"> “I moved here two weeks ago and everything still feels unfamiliar.”\n"
"\n"
"A purely practical response might be:\n"
"\n"
"> “You should download a city guide.”\n"
"\n"
"But an emotional response may connect better:\n"
"\n"
"> “That sounds exciting, but probably a little overwhelming too. How has it "
"been so far?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not always need to solve a problem. Sometimes people simply want to "
"feel understood.\n"
"\n"
"## Show That You Are Really Listening\n"
"\n"
"A useful technique is to:\n"
"\n"
"1. Ask a question.\n"
"2. Listen carefully.\n"
"3. Summarize the important part in your own words.\n"
"4. Check whether you understood correctly.\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “So you started learning photography because you wanted a creative hobby "
"outside work. Did I understand that correctly?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not need to do this formally after every answer. Short natural "
"responses also work:\n"
"\n"
"- “So that is what brought you here.”\n"
"- “It sounds like you really enjoyed it.”\n"
"- “I can see why that was frustrating.”\n"
"- “So you prefer quieter places?”\n"
"- “That makes sense.”\n"
"\n"
"These responses show that you are not simply waiting for your turn to "
"speak.\n"
"\n"
"## Share Something About Yourself Too\n"
"\n"
"Good small talk should not feel like an interview. After asking a question, "
"share a related detail about yourself.\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “What kind of music do you listen to? I’ve been listening to a lot of "
"jazz recently, but I’m trying to discover something new.”\n"
"\n"
"Or:\n"
"\n"
"> “Have you travelled anywhere interesting recently? I visited a small town "
"by a lake last weekend and really enjoyed it.”\n"
"\n"
"A useful pattern is:\n"
"\n"
"**Ask → Listen → Respond → Share → Ask again**\n"
"\n"
"This creates a balanced conversation in which both people gradually reveal "
"more about themselves.\n"
"\n"
"## How to Keep the Conversation Going\n"
"\n"
"Listen for a detail you can explore.\n"
"\n"
"If the person says:\n"
"\n"
"> “I recently started running.”\n"
"\n"
"You could ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “What made you start?”\n"
"- “Do you prefer running alone or with other people?”\n"
"- “Was it difficult in the beginning?”\n"
"- “Are you training for anything?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not need to introduce a completely new topic after every answer. "
"Often, the easiest way to continue is to become curious about one detail "
"the person has already mentioned.\n"
"\n"
"## What to Avoid\n"
"\n"
"Try not to:\n"
"\n"
"- Ask many unrelated questions one after another.\n"
"- Turn every answer back to yourself.\n"
"- Give advice before understanding what the person needs.\n"
"- Ask questions that are too personal too quickly.\n"
"- Pretend to understand when you are confused.\n"
"- Focus so much on perfect grammar that you stop listening.\n"
"\n"
"It is completely natural to pause, search for a word, or ask someone to "
"repeat themselves.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “Sorry, could you say that again?”\n"
"- “What do you mean by that?”\n"
"- “I’m not sure I understood correctly.”\n"
"- “How would you describe it?”\n"
"- “Give me a second—I’m trying to find the right word.”\n"
"\n"
"## How to End the Conversation Politely\n"
"\n"
"Not every conversation needs to last a long time. You can finish warmly "
"without making the moment awkward:\n"
"\n"
"- “It was really nice talking to you.”\n"
"- “I’m going to get another drink, but I’m glad we met.”\n"
"- “I’ll let you talk to the others, but I enjoyed our conversation.”\n"
"- “I hope you enjoy the rest of the event.”\n"
"- “Maybe I’ll see you again later.”\n"
"\n"
"Ending a conversation politely is also an important communication skill.\n"
"\n"
"## A Simple Conversation Formula\n"
"\n"
"When you are unsure what to do, remember:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Open:** Comment on the shared situation.\n"
"2. **Explore:** Ask about an experience, preference, or feeling.\n"
"3. **Listen:** Respond to something specific they said.\n"
"4. **Connect:** Share a related detail about yourself.\n"
"5. **Continue or close:** Ask a follow-up question or end politely.\n"
"\n"
"## Practice Scenario\n"
"\n"
"In this roleplay, you are meeting a friendly stranger at a social event. "
"Start with an easy question, listen for interesting details, and gradually "
"move from basic facts to experiences and interests.\n"
"\n"
"Try to practice at least three skills:\n"
"\n"
"- Ask one question about an experience or feeling.\n"
"- Refer to something the stranger said earlier.\n"
"- Share something related about yourself.\n"
"\n"
"Do not worry about creating a perfect conversation. Concentrate on being "
"curious, attentive, and easy to talk to."
msgstr ""
"Hablar con un desconocido puede ser incómodo porque ninguna de las personas "
"sabe qué decir todavía. El objetivo no es sonar especialmente ingenioso o "
"interesante. Tu primer objetivo es simplemente hacer que la otra persona se "
"sienta cómoda y descubrir algo de lo que puedan hablar juntos.\n"
"\n"
"Los consejos a continuación están inspirados en ideas del libro de Charles "
"Duhigg *Supercommunicators*.\n"
"\n"
"## Cómo Iniciar una Conversación\n"
"\n"
"Usa la situación que ya comparten. No necesitas una frase de apertura "
"original o impresionante.\n"
"\n"
"Intenta hacer una observación simple y añadir una pregunta fácil:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Este lugar está más concurrido de lo que esperaba. ¿Has estado aquí "
"antes?\"\n"
"- \"¿Cómo conoces al anfitrión?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué te trajo a este evento?\"\n"
"- \"Eso parece interesante. ¿Qué estás bebiendo?\"\n"
"- \"¿Es la primera vez que vienes a un evento como este?\"\n"
"\n"
"Las preguntas sobre la situación inmediata se sienten naturales porque dan "
"a la otra persona una forma obvia de responder.\n"
"\n"
"## Haz preguntas Que Revelen Más Que Hechos\n"
"\n"
"Las preguntas básicas son útiles para iniciar una conversación:\n"
"\n"
"- \"¿De dónde eres?\"\n"
"- \"¿A qué te dedicas?\"\n"
"- \"¿Vives cerca de aquí?\"\n"
"\n"
"Sin embargo, una conversación se vuelve más interesante cuando preguntas "
"suavemente sobre las experiencias, sentimientos o preferencias de la "
"persona.\n"
"\n"
"En lugar de solo preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿A qué te dedicas?\"\n"
"\n"
"Podrías continuar con:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Qué es lo que más disfrutas de eso?\"\n"
"\n"
"En lugar de:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Cuánto tiempo has vivido aquí?\"\n"
"\n"
"Intenta:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Qué es lo que más te gusta de vivir aquí?\"\n"
"\n"
"Otras preguntas útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- \"¿Cómo te interesaste en eso?\"\n"
"- \"¿Cuál ha sido la mejor parte de tu semana?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué sueles disfrutar hacer los fines de semana?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué es algo que esperas con ansias?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué tipo de lugares te gusta visitar?\"\n"
"\n"
"Estas preguntas son lo suficientemente personales como para crear una "
"conexión, pero todavía seguras y apropiadas para alguien que acabas de "
"conocer.\n"
"\n"
"## Escucha el Tipo de Conversación Que Quieren Tener\n"
"\n"
"Las personas no siempre quieren lo mismo de una conversación. Pueden "
"querer:\n"
"\n"
"1. Intercambiar información o tomar una decisión.\n"
"2. Compartir una emoción o experiencia.\n"
"3. Hablar sobre sus intereses, identidad o relaciones.\n"
"\n"
"Intenta responder al tipo de conversación que la otra persona ha iniciado.\n"
"\n"
"Si alguien dice:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Me mudé aquí hace dos semanas y todo todavía se siente desconocido.\"\n"
"\n"
"Una respuesta puramente práctica podría ser:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Deberías descargar una guía de la ciudad.\"\n"
"\n"
"Pero una respuesta emocional puede conectar mejor:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Eso suena emocionante, pero probablemente un poco abrumador también. "
"¿Cómo ha sido hasta ahora?\"\n"
"\n"
"No siempre necesitas resolver un problema. A veces la gente simplemente "
"quiere sentirse comprendida.\n"
"\n"
"## Muestra Que Realmente Estás Escuchando\n"
"\n"
"Una técnica útil es:\n"
"\n"
"1. Haz una pregunta.\n"
"2. Escucha atentamente.\n"
"3. Resume la parte importante con tus propias palabras.\n"
"4. Verifica si entendiste correctamente.\n"
"\n"
"Por ejemplo:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Entonces empezaste a aprender fotografía porque querías un hobby "
"creativo fuera del trabajo. ¿Entendí bien?\"\n"
"\n"
"No necesitas hacer esto formalmente después de cada respuesta. Respuestas "
"breves y naturales también funcionan:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Así que eso te trajo aquí.\"\n"
"- \"Parece que realmente lo disfrutaste.\"\n"
"- \"Puedo ver por qué eso fue frustrante.\"\n"
"- \"¿Así que prefieres lugares más tranquilos?\"\n"
"- \"Eso tiene sentido.\"\n"
"\n"
"Estas respuestas muestran que no estás simplemente esperando tu turno para "
"hablar.\n"
"\n"
"## Comparte Algo Sobre Ti También\n"
"\n"
"Una buena charla trivial no debería parecer una entrevista. Después de "
"hacer una pregunta, comparte un detalle relacionado sobre ti.\n"
"\n"
"Por ejemplo:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Qué tipo de música escuchas? He estado escuchando mucho jazz "
"últimamente, pero estoy tratando de descubrir algo nuevo.\"\n"
"\n"
"O:\n"
"\n"
"> \"¿Has viajado a algún lugar interesante últimamente? Visité un pequeño "
"pueblo junto a un lago el fin de semana pasado y realmente lo disfruté.\"\n"
"\n"
"Un patrón útil es:\n"
"\n"
"**Pregunta → Escucha → Responde → Comparte → Pregunta de nuevo**\n"
"\n"
"Esto crea una conversación equilibrada en la que ambas personas revelan "
"gradualmente más sobre sí mismas.\n"
"\n"
"## Cómo Mantener la Conversación\n"
"\n"
"Escucha un detalle que puedas explorar.\n"
"\n"
"Si la persona dice:\n"
"\n"
"> \"Recientemente empecé a correr.\"\n"
"\n"
"Podrías preguntar:\n"
"\n"
"- \"¿Qué te hizo comenzar?\"\n"
"- \"¿Prefieres correr solo o con otras personas?\"\n"
"- \"¿Fue difícil al principio?\"\n"
"- \"¿Estás entrenando para algo?\"\n"
"\n"
"No necesitas introducir un tema completamente nuevo después de cada "
"respuesta. A menudo, la manera más fácil de continuar es volverse curioso "
"sobre un detalle que la persona ya ha mencionado.\n"
"\n"
"## Qué Evitar\n"
"\n"
"Intenta no:\n"
"\n"
"- Hacer muchas preguntas no relacionadas una tras otra.\n"
"- Volver cada respuesta hacia ti mismo.\n"
"- Dar consejos antes de entender lo que la persona necesita.\n"
"- Hacer preguntas que sean demasiado personales demasiado rápido.\n"
"- Fingir entender cuando estás confundido.\n"
"- Enfocarte tanto en la gramática perfecta que dejes de escuchar.\n"
"\n"
"Es completamente natural hacer una pausa, buscar una palabra o pedirle a "
"alguien que repita.\n"
"\n"
"Frases útiles incluyen:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Perdón, ¿podrías repetir eso?\"\n"
"- \"¿Qué quieres decir con eso?\"\n"
"- \"No estoy seguro de haber entendido correctamente.\"\n"
"- \"¿Cómo lo describirías?\"\n"
"- \"Dame un segundo—estoy tratando de encontrar la palabra correcta.\"\n"
"\n"
"## Cómo Terminar la Conversación Cortésmente\n"
"\n"
"No todas las conversaciones necesitan durar mucho tiempo. Puedes terminar "
"de manera cálida sin hacer que el momento sea incómodo:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Fue realmente agradable hablar contigo.\"\n"
"- \"Voy a tomar otra bebida, pero me alegra que nos conocimos.\"\n"
"- \"Te dejaré hablar con los demás, pero disfruté nuestra conversación.\"\n"
"- \"Espero que disfrutes el resto del evento.\"\n"
"- \"Tal vez nos veamos de nuevo más tarde.\"\n"
"\n"
"Terminar una conversación cortésmente es también una habilidad de "
"comunicación importante.\n"
"\n"
"## Una Fórmula Simple de Conversación\n"
"\n"
"Cuando no estés seguro de qué hacer, recuerda:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Abrir:** Comentar sobre la situación compartida.\n"
"2. **Explorar:** Preguntar sobre una experiencia, preferencia o "
"sentimiento.\n"
"3. **Escuchar:** Responder a algo específico que dijeron.\n"
"4. **Conectar:** Compartir un detalle relacionado sobre ti mismo.\n"
"5. **Continuar o cerrar:** Hacer una pregunta de seguimiento o terminar "
"cortésmente.\n"
"\n"
"## Escenario de Práctica\n"
"\n"
"En este juego de roles, estás conociendo a un desconocido amigable en un "
"evento social. Comienza con una pregunta fácil, escucha detalles "
"interesantes y pasa gradualmente de hechos básicos a experiencias e "
"intereses.\n"
"\n"
"Intenta practicar al menos tres habilidades:\n"
"\n"
"- Haz una pregunta sobre una experiencia o sentimiento.\n"
"- Haz referencia a algo que el desconocido dijo antes.\n"
"- Comparte algo relacionado sobre ti mismo.\n"
"\n"
"No te preocupes por crear una conversación perfecta. Concéntrate en ser "
"curioso, atento y fácil de hablar."

